{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ubiquitous computing ubiquitous computing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md","fileName":"Ubiquitous Computing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ubiquitous Computing","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Human-Computer Interaction Foundations]] [[Cloud-Enabled Work and Society]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Cloud-Enabled Work and Society","Tangible User Interface","Augmented Reality","3D Interaction","Bring Your Own Device","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf","cloudcomputing_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ubiquitous computing ubiquitous computing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md","fileName":"Ubiquitous Computing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ubiquitous Computing","heading":"Ubiquitous Computing","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Ubiquitous computing is the idea that computing devices, infrastructure, and access to them can be mobile or distributed throughout the physical environment, giving people computational access across places and situations.\n\nBecause computation is no longer confined to a desktop, conventional interface components may be unsuitable. A ubiquitous system can use spatial context, embedded devices, and [[Tangible User Interface|tangible interfaces]], and may overlap with [[Augmented Reality]] or other forms of [[3D Interaction]].\n\nUbiquitous computing can remain calm and operate in the background, or it can expose an interface when interaction is needed. AR supplies one situated interface for that distributed computation by placing information directly in the physical context to which it applies.\n\nCloud services extend ubiquitous computing by making applications and organizational information available from different locations, devices, and times. This goes beyond [[Bring Your Own Device]] because the central idea is access wherever it is needed, not merely ownership of the client hardware.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Cloud-Enabled Work and Society","Tangible User Interface","Augmented Reality","3D Interaction","Bring Your Own Device","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf","cloudcomputing_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ubiquitous computing ubiquitous computing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ubiquitous Computing.md","fileName":"Ubiquitous Computing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ubiquitous Computing","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf]]\n[[augmentedreality_pearson.pdf]]\n\n[[cloudcomputing_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Cloud-Enabled Work and Society","Tangible User Interface","Augmented Reality","3D Interaction","Bring Your Own Device","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf","cloudcomputing_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}